Start here
==========

This is a Windows port of cadaver (https://github.com/notroj/cadaver).
If you are picking it up cold, read these in order:

  1. README.md    what this fork is, how to build it, how to run it
  2. AGENTS.md    how to drive cadaver from a program, and how to read
                  what it prints back -- including the --json contract
  3. PATCHES.md   what was changed inside neon/ and why, needed before
                  you update the bundled neon
  4. This file    what is left to do

Layout:

  src/            cadaver itself: the command implementations
                  (commands.c), the command table and dispatcher
                  (cadaver.c), the tokeniser and glob driver
                  (cmdline.c), options (options.c), listings (ls.c),
                  the edit command (edit.c), DASL search (search.c),
                  DeltaV (version.c), and output.c, which decides where
                  everything cadaver prints goes and records how each
                  command went
  lib/            portability: the platform layer (system.c), filename
                  globbing (glob.c), the password prompt (getpass.c),
                  temporary files (mkstemp.c), .netrc (netrc.c)
  neon/           vendored neon 0.37.1, checked in, not a submodule.
                  Patched; see PATCHES.md
  win32/config.h  generated config.h, checked in so Makefile.w32 works
                  without autotools.  win32/regen-config.sh rewrites it
  VERSION         the fork version, read by both build paths
  tests/          the five test harnesses and their expected output
  Makefile.w32    the no-autotools build
  doc/cadaver.1   the manual page
  .github/        CI: lint, both build paths, five gates, release

Everything is one executable:

  cadaver URL
  cadaver -r commands.txt URL
  cadaver --json --trace=wire.log -r commands.txt URL
  printf 'ls\nquit\n' | cadaver URL

Test harnesses in tests/:

  offline.sh      43 checks that need no server, no network and no
                  Python.  Run it first; it takes under a second.  It
                  also greps src/ for a write to standard output that
                  does not go through output.c, which is the one rule
                  that keeps --json a document rather than a document
                  with a stray line in it.
  glob.sh         48 unit tests for lib/glob.c, compiled on the spot.
  netrc.sh        26 unit tests for lib/netrc.c, likewise.  What that
                  parser gets wrong is invisible in use, which is how
                  upstream #75 survived four years.
  godav.sh        the scripted sessions against golang.org/x/net/webdav
  wsgidav.sh      the same sessions against wsgidav
  session.sh      the driver both of those call: runs each
                  tests/sessions/*.cad and compares the transcript
                  against tests/expected/<server>/
  normalise.py    replaces the parts of a transcript, or of a --json
                  document, that legitimately differ between runs
  expand.py       substitutes the paths into a session script

A session is tests/sessions/NAME.cad, and may bring:

  NAME.flags      extra arguments for the cadaver command line
  NAME.home       a .netrc, in a home directory of the session's own
  NAME.stdin      what to feed standard input
  NAME.servers    the servers it applies to, one per line
  NAME.check      a shell snippet run afterwards with $WORK set, for
                  what a transcript cannot show

Every session's exit status is pinned on the last line of its
transcript, because that is the thing a script branches on and a
transcript cannot otherwise show it.

Neither test server is complete, and the difference matters:

  * x/net/webdav locks correctly, including a LOCK on an unmapped URL,
    but has no dead property store, so most of the props session fails
    against it.  tests/godav.sh also puts authentication in front of one
    subtree and makes another ignore Range, because no real server here
    does either; the two netrc sessions and the norange one run only
    there.
  * wsgidav has a dead property store, so props is meaningful there,
    but it answers LOCK with the Content-Type "application;
    charset=utf-8", which is not a media type.  neon discards the body
    unparsed, cadaver never sees the lock token, and every lock test
    reports a failure that is the server's.

So: verify lock work against godav.sh, property work against
wsgidav.sh, and do not read either result as the whole picture.

Before you change anything
==========================

Run the gates and note the numbers:

  ./tests/offline.sh
  ./tests/glob.sh
  ./tests/netrc.sh
  ./tests/godav.sh
  ./tests/wsgidav.sh

All five must pass, the last two printing "All sessions match the
expected results".  If they do not, fix that before doing anything
else.  Everything below assumes a green baseline.

Both build paths must stay warning-free:

  ./autogen.sh && ./configure --with-ssl=openssl --enable-warnings && make
  make -f Makefile.w32

CI greps the build log for "warning:" and fails on a hit, so a new
warning is a regression rather than background noise.

Conventions, in code and in prose:

  * Binary units, always: KiB, MiB, GiB, powers of 1024.  Never KB, MB
    or GB for a quantity of bytes, and never a decimal power of ten.
    Rates are MiB/s.
  * Timestamps in anything machine-readable are ISO 8601 in UTC with a
    trailing Z: millisecond precision for a clock reading cadaver took
    itself, second precision for one it got from a server, which does
    not offer more.  Truncate sub-second digits, do not round, so a
    stamp never names a moment that had not happened yet.
  * Durations are seconds to millisecond resolution, and are
    wall-clock.  Say so when reporting them, because they include
    server and network time.
  * Quote exact byte counts where the exact figure matters, with the
    rounded binary figure in brackets after it.
  * The version lives in VERSION and nowhere else.  win32/config.h
    deliberately has no copy of it.
  * Nothing in src/ calls printf, putchar or puts.  Everything goes
    through out_printf() and friends in src/output.c, because with
    --json standard output carries the result document and one stray
    write would corrupt it.  tests/offline.sh checks.

If you change what cadaver prints, the expected transcripts have to
move with it:

  ./tests/godav.sh --regenerate
  ./tests/wsgidav.sh --regenerate

and then read the diff before committing it.  --regenerate will
happily enshrine a regression; the point of those files is that a
change in behaviour has to be looked at by a person.

High value work
===============

In rough order.

* No recursive upload or download.  `rget' and `rput', creating
  collections as needed, are the most-requested missing feature and the
  reason people reach for sitecopy or rclone instead.  `mput *' on a
  directory now says what it is rather than blaming the permissions,
  which is honest but is not the feature.

* Ctrl-C during a transfer kills cadaver.  It should abort the transfer
  and return to the prompt.  The signal handler in src/cadaver.c exits
  unless a child is running; neon has no cancel call, so this needs the
  read callback to return an error, which means a flag the transfer
  loop checks.  Interrupting a remote wildcard expansion already works
  this way -- see interrupt_state in src/cmdline.c.

* `copy' and `move' over an existing resource fail with 412 rather than
  prompting or reporting clearly.  The `overwrite' option decides
  whether the Overwrite header is T or F; a 412 back from F deserves a
  message saying that the destination exists and that `set overwrite
  on' is how to replace it.

* A resource-state cache for the duration of one command.  cadaver
  makes a PROPFIND to decide whether a path is a collection, and does
  it repeatedly within one command: `copy a b c dest' resolves the
  destination once and every source separately.  A small hash keyed by
  URI, discarded at the end of each command, would cut the request
  count noticeably on a slow link.  uri_resolve_native_true() in
  src/commands.c is the one place that asks.

* `ls' is inflexible: one format, one collection at a time, no way to
  choose which properties are shown.  --json now covers the machine
  side of this, so what is left is a `set lsformat' for a person.

* DASL search and the DeltaV commands are exercised by neither test
  server, because neither implements them.  They are compiled and
  linked and otherwise unverified.  A third test server that does would
  be the way to cover them; until then, treat any change there as
  untested.  Apache httpd with mod_dav and mod_dav_fs would cover
  DeltaV badly and DASL not at all, so this probably means writing one,
  the way tests/godav is written.

* Upload on save from the editor, so that `edit' with a long-running
  editor pushes each save rather than only the final one.  Requested in
  2001 and never done; a file watcher would do it now.

Smaller things
==============

* Failure paths leak.  Most commands build URLs with ne_concat and
  return early without freeing them.  It is memory only, in a process
  that is about to read another command rather than exit, so a long
  session with many failures grows.  Cleaning it up properly means a
  cleanup path per command.

* `lls' accepts only -l.  It is cadaver's own listing now rather than a
  forked ls, so anything else -- -a, -t, -S -- has to be implemented
  rather than passed on.  -a is the one worth having: the listing hides
  nothing today, so -a would have to become the non-default.

* The progress indicator prints one dot per transfer callback when the
  output is not a terminal, so a large transfer produces a line of
  hundreds of dots in a log.  --json suppresses it entirely and
  tests/normalise.py collapses it for the expected transcripts.
  Consider a byte count instead when the output is not a terminal.

* `set searchdepth' silently accepts anything it does not recognise and
  treats it as infinity, where every other option with a fixed set of
  values says so and fails.

* The completion scope is a string in the command table, one letter per
  argument.  The globbing scope is still one value for the whole
  command, which is why `put *.txt' does not expand and `mput *.txt'
  does.  Making globbing per-argument as well would be consistent, but
  it would change what `put *.txt' does, so it wants a release note
  rather than a quiet fix.

* An option name completes after `set' and `describe' according to the
  table, but nothing implements it: completion() in src/cadaver.c has
  parmscope_option falling through to no matches.  A generator over the
  options table is a dozen lines.

Inherited upstream issues
=========================

Every open issue in notroj/cadaver at the time of the fork.  All five
that were bugs are fixed here; upstream has not moved on any of them.

* #75, netrc parser removes single quote from value (open since
  2026-04-27).  FIXED in 0.28-win2.  lib/netrc.c treated a quote as
  opening a quoted run wherever it met one, so a value containing one
  lost it and everything after it ran together.  A quote now quotes
  only when it opens a value.  tests/netrc.sh covers it, and the
  `netrc' session authenticates against the test server with a password
  that has an apostrophe in it.

* #64, lock owner is not XML-escaped (open since 2025-05-29).  FIXED in
  0.28-win2.  getowner() in src/commands.c escapes the value before it
  goes into the <owner> element, because neon concatenates it into the
  request body verbatim and has no escaping helper to call.  The
  `lockowner' session sets an owner containing & < > and " and checks
  the escaped form on the wire through --trace.

  The same escaping arguably belongs in neon's ne_locks.c, which would
  fix it for every neon client.  Worth reporting there.

* #54, `mv collection/ newname/' fails (open since 2024-11-27, reported
  by the upstream maintainer).  FIXED in 0.28-win2.  do_copymove() now
  distinguishes a destination that exists and is not a collection,
  which is still refused, from one that does not exist, which is a
  rename.  The `mvcoll' session covers all three shapes.

* #25, automatic login with .cadaverrc and .netrc not working (open
  since 2023-01-10).  FIXED in 0.28-win2.  An entry with a login and no
  password is used for the login and only the password is asked for.
  The `netrclogin' session covers it end to end.

* #4, cannot access a share whose resourcetype is malformed (open since
  2022-07-15).  FIXED in 0.28-win2, as far as it can be: the server is
  wrong and the reporter could not get it fixed, so what was left was
  the message.  set_path() in src/cadaver.c now tells "the PROPFIND
  failed" apart from "the PROPFIND succeeded and the resource did not
  report itself as a collection", and the second says so and points at
  -t.

* #58 is not a bug report; it is an offer of help from a university
  open source programme office.  Nothing to do.

Upstream pull requests
======================

* #74, "Misc changes", open.  Two of its three parts are taken: the
  neon update to 0.37.1 and propname_iterator()'s bullet.  The third is
  a ne_version_match(0, 29) check at startup, which with a statically
  linked bundled neon can never fire; take it if the tree ever grows a
  way to build against a system neon.

  Every other pull request upstream is merged or closed.

Things that were looked at and deliberately not done
====================================================

* Keeping NLS.  cadaver had Spanish and Italian translations and a
  gettext build.  A single self-contained executable cannot carry a
  locale directory, and a translated build would need the .mo files
  installed next to it, which is the opposite of what this fork is for.
  The _() and N_() macros are still in the source and resolve to the
  identity, so no message has been lost.  Bringing it back means
  restoring the po directory and its Makefile.in.in from upstream's
  history, the gettext and iconv macros in m4, AM_GNU_GETTEXT in
  configure.ac, and a LOCALEDIR for src/cadaver.c to hand
  bindtextdomain() -- that call is still there under #ifdef ENABLE_NLS
  and would not compile without one.

* Keeping the bundled glibc glob.  It is 35707 bytes (34.9 KiB) of
  1998 glibc, needs fnmatch, pwd.h and a d_type that Windows does not
  have, and its interrupt support was inside #ifdef SHELL, which was
  never defined -- so the Ctrl-C handling cadaver installed around it
  had never worked.  lib/glob.c is a third the size, has unit tests,
  and handles Windows separators and case-insensitivity.

* Emitting LF rather than CRLF on standard output.  cadaver writes its
  output in text mode, so a Windows console and a Windows editor both
  see what they expect.  Transfers are unaffected: `put', `get' and
  `edit' are binary throughout, and `cat' switches its own output to
  binary for the length of the transfer.  tests/normalise.py strips
  carriage returns, so the expected transcripts do not depend on it.

* Sorting `ls' output differently.  ls.c sorts errors first, then
  collections, then by URI, which is what upstream does and what the
  expected transcripts record.  Changing it is a user-visible choice
  that should be a `set' option rather than a new default.

* An MSVC build.  Everything here assumes GCC: the warning set, the
  __attribute__ annotations in neon, the makefiles.  MinGW-w64 produces
  a native Windows binary with no runtime dependency on MSYS2, which is
  what the port needed; MSVC would add a second toolchain to support
  for no gain the users can see.

* JSON Lines rather than one object.  litmus-windows emits one object
  per suite, so a run of several is JSON Lines.  A cadaver process is
  one session, so there is one object and nothing to stream; making it
  a line-oriented format would only make the common case harder to
  read.

* Removing the truncation `resumeget' does when its request fails.
  TODO used to say that neon appended a whole second copy of the
  resource when a server ignored the Range header, because
  ne_get_range() checks the status after the body could have been
  written.  It does not: dispatch_to_fd() below it writes to the
  descriptor only for a 206 and discards anything else, which the
  `norange' session established by arranging a server that ignores
  Range.  The truncation is kept because "a failed resumeget leaves the
  local file as it was" is worth guaranteeing here rather than by
  inspection of a library this tree is expected to update.

* Tracing the body of a transfer.  --trace carries every message body
  except the resource itself during `put', `get', `mput', `mget',
  `cat' and `edit'.  A trace is for looking at the protocol, and a
  100 MiB upload in a log file helps nobody.  If it is ever wanted,
  out_start_transfer() in src/output.c is the one place that turns it
  off.

Cutting a release
=================

Bump VERSION, regenerate the checked-in configuration if the neon
version or anything else configure records has moved:

  ./win32/regen-config.sh

commit, wait for CI to go green on that commit, then

  git tag -a vX.Y-winN
  git push origin vX.Y-winN

The tag must be annotated and its name must be "v" plus VERSION
exactly, or the release job refuses to run.  It also refuses if a
release with that tag already exists, and it checks both before it
uploads anything, so a good release cannot be overwritten by accident.
The workflow copies the tag's message above the generated notes, so the
tag message is the release description.

Do not quote the built executable's size in a tag message: it is not
known until CI has built it, and it moves with the OpenSSL, expat and
readline versions the runner happens to have.

Afterwards, confirm the fixed URL resolves and the binary runs:

  curl -fsSL -o cadaver.exe https://github.com/Azathothas/cadaver-windows/releases/latest/download/cadaver.exe
  ./cadaver.exe --version
